<h2 data-section-id=3D"1poqhhs" data-start=3D"42" data-end=3D"65">The three=
 coil types</h2>
<h3 data-section-id=3D"tngdkj" data-start=3D"67" data-end=3D"89"><span role=
=3D"text">1. <strong data-start=3D"74" data-end=3D"89">Flat spiral</strong>=
</span></h3>
<p data-start=3D"90" data-end=3D"205">This is the <strong data-start=3D"102=
" data-end=3D"129">flat pancake-style coil</strong>. The wire winds in one =
plane, from the center outward. You often see it in:</p>
<ul data-start=3D"206" data-end=3D"256">
<li data-section-id=3D"svuwtn" data-start=3D"206" data-end=3D"224">
<p data-start=3D"208" data-end=3D"224">flat applicators</p>
</li>
<li data-section-id=3D"1h5t44j" data-start=3D"225" data-end=3D"236">
<p data-start=3D"227" data-end=3D"236">PCB coils</p>
</li>
<li data-section-id=3D"oofudu" data-start=3D"237" data-end=3D"256">
<p data-start=3D"239" data-end=3D"256">shallow PEMF pads</p>
</li>
</ul>
<p data-start=3D"258" data-end=3D"304">The first two images show this style=
 clearly.</p>
<h3 data-section-id=3D"9w4imc" data-start=3D"306" data-end=3D"327"><span ro=
le=3D"text">2. <strong data-start=3D"313" data-end=3D"327">Round loop</stro=
ng></span></h3>
<p data-start=3D"328" data-end=3D"621">This is basically one or more <stron=
g data-start=3D"358" data-end=3D"376">circular loops</strong> of wire, but =
not stretched into a long cylinder like a solenoid. It is simpler than a so=
lenoid and often used when you want a field centered around the loop area. =
The third image is close to this geometry, though it is shown as multiple t=
urns.</p>
<h3 data-section-id=3D"6g5n7m" data-start=3D"623" data-end=3D"642"><span ro=
le=3D"text">3. <strong data-start=3D"630" data-end=3D"642">Solenoid</strong=
></span></h3>
<p data-start=3D"643" data-end=3D"828">This is the <strong data-start=3D"65=
5" data-end=3D"680">long cylindrical coil</strong> shape, like a spring wra=
pped around a tube or core. It is the classic electromagnet coil shape. The=
 fourth image shows a typical solenoid clearly.</p>
